        <OtherAbstract Type="PIP" Language="eng">
            <AbstractText>There are about 17 million IUD users in the world, excluding China. Tolerance and effectiveness of the device depends very much on its size, and on its proper adaptation into the uterine cavity. Copper potentiated IUDs have practically supplanted inert IUDs; their mode of action is at an endometrial level, but they also exert a spermatotoxic action. Prostaglandins seem to play an important role in the mechanism of action of copper IUDs. Side effects are bleeding, due to an increase in capillary permeability, and pain. Complications include infection and uterine perforation. Progesterone-releasing IUDs help reduce excessive menstrual bleeding, but they cannot be used for longer than 1 year.</AbstractText>
        </OtherAbstract>
